Upstream's fix for a heap overflow in zlib's gzip file writer, on top of zlib v1.3.2.
Applied with "git apply" by native/CMakeLists.txt; git apply ignores this header.

Fixes: CVE-2026-76844, CVE-2026-85091

After a write fails (or stalls, for a non-blocking descriptor), gz_write() returned with
strm.next_in still pointing into the caller's buffer, and the next gz* write call could write past
the end of zlib's own input buffer. The fix resets the input pointer before returning.

This is madler/zlib commit df84af25dc1942490e1d1c899a07619152a46148 (Mark Adler, 2026-09-16,
"Fix buffer overflow bug in non-blocking gzwrite."), unchanged. Upstream's message names
CVE-2026-85091; OSV lists the same commit as the fix for CVE-2026-76844. (GitHub's advisory
database maps CVE-2026-76844 to GHSA-g84c-rxfj-3j2c, which is a webpack-dev-middleware advisory:
a mistake on their side, so don't go by that GHSA.) Drop this patch when zlib releases a version
after 1.3.2 that includes it (none had by 2026-10-11).

In the app, readsb doesn't reach gzwrite (no gzip JSON, globe index, heatmap or state files), so
this closes the advisory rather than a live path.

tools/security-scan.ps1 treats the advisories on the "Fixes:" line as fixed for this library.

diff --git a/gzwrite.c b/gzwrite.c
index b217b85ce..512329244 100644
--- a/gzwrite.c
+++ b/gzwrite.c
@@ -242,8 +242,11 @@ local z_size_t gz_write(gz_statep state, voidpc buf, z_size_t len) {
             n -= state->strm.avail_in;
             state->x.pos += n;
             len -= n;
-            if (ret == -1)
+            if (ret == -1) {
+                state->strm.avail_in = 0;
+                state->strm.next_in = state->in;
                 return state->again ? put - len : 0;
+            }
         } while (len);
     }
 
